The main forces on a rising bubble are buoyancy and drag. Buoyancy is given by \(F_b = \rho_\ell \frac{4}{3}\pi a^3 g_0\), and drag by Stokes' law \(F_d = 6\pi \eta a v\). At terminal velocity, these balance: \(\rho_\ell \frac{4}{3}\pi a^3 g_0 = 6\pi \eta a v\), yielding the velocity expression \(v(a) = \frac{2\rho_\ell g_0 a^2}{9\eta}\).

From Figure 3, the radius of the penultimate bubble is \(a = 0.19\,\mathrm{mm} = 1.9 \times 10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m}\). Bubbles are emitted at frequency \(f_b = 20\,\mathrm{Hz}\), so the time interval between emissions is \(\Delta t = 0.05\,\mathrm{s}\). The distance between the antepenultimate and last bubble is \(4.5\,\mathrm{mm}\); assuming equal spacing, the separation between consecutive bubbles is half this, \(2.25\,\mathrm{mm}\). Thus, the velocity is \(v = \frac{2.25 \times 10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m}}{0.05\,\mathrm{s}} = 0.045\,\mathrm{m/s}\). Using \(\rho_\ell = 1.0 \times 10^3\,\mathrm{kg/m^3}\) and \(g_0 = 9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}\), the viscosity is calculated as \(\eta = \frac{2\rho_\ell g_0 a^2}{9v} \approx 1.75 \times 10^{-3}\,\mathrm{Pa\cdot s}\).

